Brokerage and other charges on stock trades
Why did I receive less than expected after selling shares?

=E2=80=A2 Brokerage is charged on every executed order, both buy and sell: =
=E2=82=B920 or 0.1% of the trade value, whichever is lower. If that is below =
=E2=82=B95, the minimum is the lower of =E2=82=B95 or 2.5% of the trade value.
=E2=80=A2 Because brokerage is per order, buying and selling a stock are two =
separate charges, and several orders in one day can add up to more than =E2=
=82=B920. F&O orders are a flat =E2=82=B920 each.
=E2=80=A2 Selling shares from your demat account adds a DP charge of =E2=82=
=B920 per sell order (=E2=82=B93.50 depository + =E2=82=B916.50 Groww; =E2=82=
=B919.75 for women investors), plus GST. Groww's =E2=82=B916.50 part is waive=
d when the sale value is below =E2=82=B9100.
=E2=80=A2 The DP charge applies per sell order, not per share: selling the sa=
me stock in two separate orders means two DP charges.
=E2=80=A2 Government and exchange charges are added on top. For delivery trad=
es: STT of 0.1% on buy and sell, stamp duty of 0.015% on buy, exchange transa=
ction charges (0.00297% on NSE), SEBI turnover fee (0.0001%), and 18% GST on =
brokerage and charges.
=E2=80=A2 Your contract note lists the exact charges for each trade, and the =
Brokerage Calculator on Groww's pricing page estimates them before you place =
an order.

